# Plan-Delivery

{{PREAMBLE}}

## Overview

`/plan-delivery` is the Factory's delivery-planning pass — it turns a settled `PRD.md` (plus the
`.factory/stack.yaml` the architecture pass wrote) into an ordered, tracked backlog of shippable
**increments**, recorded in a committed `PLAN.md` beside the PRD. Each increment is a slice of value
that traces back to a PRD goal, carries an effort and estimated-token field, and moves through a
fixed lifecycle (`todo → in-progress → shipped`). The build loop binds one increment at a time;
`/ship` flips exactly one to `shipped` per loop and records its actual token spend.

It is a *wrapper*: it composes `project-planner` for the genuine work breakdown — milestones,
dependencies, sequencing — and `sprint-planner` for increment sizing and committing a realistic
backlog, and adds the Factory's delivery discipline: goal traceability, a single active increment,
the PLAN → BUILD sign-off gate, and a cost ledger (estimated vs actual tokens) so the estimates
calibrate against real usage over time.

## When to Activate

Activate when:
- A `PRD.md` is settled (`status: in-design` or `in-build`) and `.factory/stack.yaml` exists, and
  the product needs a delivery sequence before the build loop starts.
- The user asks "what do we build first", "break this into increments", "plan the roadmap",
  "what's the delivery order", or "how do we phase this".

**Do not activate** (adjacent skills own this):
- `plan-product` — decides *what/why* (is this the right product/scope); `/plan-delivery` is
  downstream and takes the settled scope as given.
- `plan-arch` — owns the tech stack and architecture in `stack.yaml`; `/plan-delivery` sequences the
  *work*, not the *stack*.
- `spec` — turns ONE increment into a precise, buildable spec; `/plan-delivery` produces the ordered
  set of increments `/spec` then sharpens one at a time.
- `project-planner` (craft) — the work-breakdown engine this wrapper composes; `/plan-delivery`
  frames it as a committed, goal-traced, gate-bearing PLAN.md.
- `sprint-planner` (craft) — the increment-sizing / realistic-backlog engine this wrapper composes;
  `/plan-delivery` uses it to size increments, not to run a sprint ceremony.

## Core Concepts

- **PLAN.md is the artifact.** A committed, human-owned file beside `PRD.md`. Its frontmatter is the
  machine source of truth (the verifier reads it); the body is the human narrative. It doubles as
  the token cost ledger.
- **Every increment traces to a PRD goal.** `goals:` cites goal ids from `PRD.md` §3. An increment
  serving no goal is scope creep and fails the verifier (`orphan-increment`).
- **One increment is active at a time.** Statuses are `todo | in-progress | shipped`; at most one is
  `in-progress`. `order` defines the delivery sequence and must be unique.
- **The PLAN → BUILD boundary is a hard sign-off gate.** The first build run stays blocked until the
  operator approves the delivery plan — the one place a human commits to the sequence before effort
  is spent.
- **PLAN.md is a cost ledger.** Each increment carries `effort` (human-team → AI-assisted) and
  `est_tokens`; once shipped, `/ship` writes `actual_tokens` from `run.json`. The gap recalibrates
  the next estimate.
- **Forward-only advance.** Status moves forward (`todo → in-progress → shipped`). Re-opening a
  shipped increment requires an explicit `reopened: true` flag — a deliberate, recorded backward move.

## Workflow

Freedom level: **medium** — the PLAN.md shape and the rules are fixed; the decomposition is yours.

1. **Read the settled inputs.** `PRD.md` (goals §3, features §10) and `.factory/stack.yaml`
   (components, commands). Note dependencies between features.
2. **List the PRD goals.** Extract the goal ids/summaries into `prd_goals:` — the traceability
   anchor every increment must cite.
3. **Decompose into increments (`project-planner`).** Slice the PRD into the smallest shippable
   increments that each deliver value; sequence them by dependency and value. Give each a unique
   `id` and `order`.
4. **Size, trace and estimate (`sprint-planner`).** Size each increment so it is realistically
   shippable in one build loop, then set `goals:` (the PRD goal ids it serves), an `effort`
   estimate, and `est_tokens`. An increment that traces to no goal is scope creep — drop it or find
   the goal it serves; an increment too big for one loop should be split.
5. **Set the initial statuses.** A fresh backlog is all `todo`; mark the first increment
   `in-progress` when the build loop is about to start it. At most one `in-progress`.
6. **Write PLAN.md** (from `templates/PLAN.template.md`) and record the run artifact. Delivery
   planning follows the architecture pass, so it is a **branch artifact** under step 2 — the next
   free sub-sequence after plan-design (`02a`) and spec (`02b`):
   ```bash
   fac run artifact --seq 2c --step plan-delivery \
     --inputs PRD.md .factory/stack.yaml --body-file plan-delivery.md
   ```
7. **Gate the hand-off.** The PLAN → BUILD boundary is a hard sign-off gate: the first build run
   stays blocked until the operator approves the plan.
8. **Hand off.** The bound increment goes to `/spec` (to sharpen) and the build loop (to implement);
   `/ship` advances the increment and records its actual token spend back into PLAN.md.

## Practical Guidance

- Slice by shippable value, not by layer — an increment that ships a thin end-to-end path beats one
  that finishes the whole backend but ships nothing.
- Keep the first increment small enough to prove the pipeline end to end.
- Sequence by dependency: an increment that another blocks comes first.
- Name the PRD goal each increment serves out loud; if you can't, it is scope creep.
- Estimate honestly and revisit: the estimated-vs-actual token gap on shipped increments is the
  signal that recalibrates the next estimate.

## Examples

**Example:**
```
Input:  PRD.md (Repair Tracker) — goals G1 "nothing falls through the cracks", G2 "a single answer
        to what is outstanding"; stack.yaml with api/web/reminders/mobile components.
Work breakdown (project-planner): INC-1 core tracking (log/assign/status/list) → G1,G2;
        INC-2 SLA reminders → G1; INC-3 contractor mobile app → G1.
Output: PLAN.md — three ordered increments, each tracing to a goal, with effort + est_tokens.
        INC-1 in-progress; the PLAN → BUILD sign-off gates the first build.
Handoff → /spec (sharpen INC-1) → build loop; /ship ships INC-1, records actual_tokens, binds INC-2.
```

## Guidelines

1. Decompose, don't re-scope — take the settled PRD as given; sequence the work, don't reopen what.
2. Every increment cites at least one real PRD goal; drop orphans.
3. Keep exactly one increment `in-progress`; `order` is unique and defines the sequence.
4. Set the PLAN → BUILD sign-off as a hard gate before the first build.
5. Record `effort` + `est_tokens` per increment; let `/ship` fill `actual_tokens`.
6. Write PLAN.md and record the run artifact (`02c-plan-delivery.md`); hand the bound increment to
   `/spec` and the build loop.

## Gotchas

1. **Orphan increments**: an increment tracing to no PRD goal is scope creep — the verifier fails it.
2. **Two active increments**: more than one `in-progress` makes "what's next" ambiguous — keep one.
3. **Slicing by layer**: a "finish the backend" increment ships nothing; slice by shippable value.
4. **Re-scoping the PRD**: deciding *what* to build is `/plan-product`; stay on the delivery order.
5. **Silent backward moves**: reverting a shipped increment without `reopened: true` is a lost audit
   trail — record the deliberate re-open.

## Integration

- `plan-arch` — writes `stack.yaml`; `/plan-delivery` reads it to sequence the work.
- `plan-product` — settles the scope `/plan-delivery` decomposes.
- `project-planner` (craft) — supplies the work breakdown, dependencies, and sequencing.
- `sprint-planner` (craft) — supplies increment sizing and a realistic, committable backlog.
- `spec` — receives one increment at a time to turn into a buildable spec.
- `ship` — advances the bound increment to `shipped` and records its actual token spend in PLAN.md.
- Run harness (`fac run`) — records the plan as `02c-plan-delivery.md`; the PLAN → BUILD sign-off is
  a hard gate (`gateTier({ signoff: true })`).
- Verifier — `lib/delivery-plan.ts` checks PLAN.md is well-formed and only advances forward.

